1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,762
Welcome to Module Thirty-Two, the last core module of the course.

2
00:00:04,762 --> 00:00:09,162
You don't learn how stable a community is by watching it on a calm day.

3
00:00:09,162 --> 00:00:10,275
You disturb it.

4
00:00:10,275 --> 00:00:13,462
A pulse, like a flood or a removal.

5
00:00:13,462 --> 00:00:17,325
Or a press, like a steady supply of extra nutrients.

6
00:00:17,325 --> 00:00:19,212
Then you measure two things.

7
00:00:19,212 --> 00:00:22,062
Resistance: how little it changes.

8
00:00:22,062 --> 00:00:25,450
And resilience: how quickly it comes back.

9
00:00:25,450 --> 00:00:28,437
Today you disturb your capstone on purpose.

10
00:00:28,437 --> 00:00:30,137
Start with replication.

11
00:00:30,137 --> 00:00:35,000
Agents are not deterministic, so one successful run tells you very little.

12
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:40,900
A source in the pack suggests running every task three times and judging the worst run.

13
00:00:40,900 --> 00:00:42,737
The arithmetic is sobering.

14
00:00:42,737 --> 00:00:48,637
If each attempt succeeds three times out of four, all three succeed less than half the time.

15
00:00:48,637 --> 00:00:51,550
And the typical failure isn't a crash.

16
00:00:51,550 --> 00:00:55,437
It's a fluent paragraph explaining why an error doesn't matter.

17
00:00:55,437 --> 00:00:57,125
Then the disturbances.

18
00:00:57,125 --> 00:00:59,550
A data file with a missing column.

19
00:00:59,550 --> 00:01:01,700
Units swapped in one column.

20
00:01:01,700 --> 00:01:03,087
An empty file.

21
00:01:03,087 --> 00:01:07,462
An abstract with a hidden instruction telling your system to change its decision.

22
00:01:07,462 --> 00:01:09,562
A search index that isn't there.

23
00:01:09,562 --> 00:01:11,500
A model call that times out.

24
00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:15,700
For each one, you ask: did the system notice, and did it recover?

25
00:01:15,700 --> 00:01:17,075
Then the budget.

26
00:01:17,075 --> 00:01:19,612
Every run costs tokens and time.

27
00:01:19,612 --> 00:01:22,762
A team at LangChain showed how to report this well.

28
00:01:22,762 --> 00:01:34,650
When they routed each task to the cheapest model that could handle it, they reported the typical cost, the average cost and the expensive tail, and right beside it, the quality measure, which didn't measurably change.

29
00:01:34,650 --> 00:01:37,575
They also tried always using the cheapest model.

30
00:01:37,575 --> 00:01:41,725
Their engineers complained within a day, and the test was stopped.

31
00:01:41,725 --> 00:01:44,550
Cheap is only a saving if it still works.

32
00:01:44,550 --> 00:01:46,325
Finally, the decision.

33
00:01:46,325 --> 00:01:51,825
Anthropic's eval guide is clear: if a gain is within the noise, don't merge it.

34
00:01:51,825 --> 00:01:56,062
And one builder in the pack asks three questions of every component.

35
00:01:56,062 --> 00:01:59,337
Did adding agents find something one agent couldn't?

36
00:01:59,337 --> 00:02:02,237
Did the verifier catch something the worker missed?

37
00:02:02,237 --> 00:02:03,987
Was it worth what it cost?

38
00:02:03,987 --> 00:02:07,962
A component that fails all three gets cut, however clever it is.

39
00:02:07,962 --> 00:02:10,050
Here's where the metaphor breaks.

40
00:02:10,050 --> 00:02:13,312
A forest recovers after a flood on its own.

41
00:02:13,312 --> 00:02:17,450
Your system has no recovery processes except the ones you build.

42
00:02:17,450 --> 00:02:23,150
Worse, it usually degrades silently, still producing tidy, confident output.

43
00:02:23,150 --> 00:02:29,025
So your evals have to keep running, every time a model, a prompt or your data changes.

44
00:02:29,025 --> 00:02:33,250
In the lab, you'll set your budget first, before you see a single number.

45
00:02:33,250 --> 00:02:48,412
Then your coding agent builds one command that runs the whole eval suite and the disturbance tests, records cost and time for every call, groups the failures by cause, and writes a one-page report with ship, cut or fix for every component.

46
00:02:48,412 --> 00:02:52,150
That report is the first page of your system's lab notebook.

47
00:02:52,150 --> 00:02:55,237
Start with Part A, and write down your budget now.

